# Arepas (Reina Pepiada and Con Queso)

Based on [Panning the Globe](https://www.panningtheglobe.com/reina-pepiada-arepas-chicken-avocado/), [Minimalist Baker](https://minimalistbaker.com/how-to-make-arepas/), and [Immaculate Bites](https://www.africanbites.com/arepas/)

{ rating="0" image="None" }
:::
:::

## Ingredients

### Arepa Dough (makes 6-8 arepas)

- [ ] 2 cups [masarepa]{ ing="masarepa" } (pre-cooked white corn flour; see Notes for sourcing)
- [ ] 2 to 2.5 [cups warm water]{ ing="cups-warm-water" }
- [ ] 1 tsp [salt]{ ing="salt" }
- [ ] 1 Tbsp [neutral oil]{ ing="neutral-oil" } or butter (optional, for richer/more tender arepas)

### Reina Pepiada Filling (chicken-avocado)

- [ ] 2 cups [shredded cooked chicken]{ ing="shredded-cooked-chicken" } (rotisserie works well)
- [ ] 2 [ripe avocados]{ ing="ripe-avocados" }
- [ ] 3 Tbsp [mayonnaise]{ ing="mayonnaise" }
- [ ] [Juice of 1 lime]{ ing="juice-of-1-lime" }
- [ ] 1/4 cup fresh [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" }, chopped
- [ ] 2 Tbsp [red onion]{ ing="red-onion" }, finely minced
- [ ] 1/2 [jalapeño]{ ing="jalape-o" }, seeded and minced (optional)
- [ ] [Salt and pepper]{ ing="salt-and-pepper" } to taste

### Con Queso Filling

- [ ] 1.5 cups [queso blanco]{ ing="queso-blanco" } or low-moisture mozzarella, shredded (see Notes for options)
- [ ] [Butter for spreading]{ ing="butter-for-spreading" } (optional)

## Recipe

### Make the Dough

1. Combine [masarepa]{ ing="masarepa" }, [salt]{ ing="salt" }, and [oil]{ ing="neutral-oil" } in a bowl
1. Add [warm water]{ ing="cups-warm-water" } gradually, mixing with hands until a smooth pliable dough forms — it should not crack at the edges when pressed flat
1. Rest 5 minutes (dough stiffens slightly; add a splash of [water]{ ing="cups-warm-water" } if too dry after resting)
1. Divide into 6-8 equal portions and press each between palms into a disc ~3/4 inch thick and 3-4 inches wide

### Cook the Arepas (Cast Iron + Oven)

1. Preheat oven to 375°F
1. Heat a cast iron skillet over medium heat with a thin film of [oil]{ ing="neutral-oil" }
1. Cook arepas 4-5 minutes per side until a golden-brown crust forms and the edges look dry
1. Transfer the cast iron to the oven and bake 10-12 minutes until arepas sound hollow when tapped
1. Remove and cool 2 minutes before splitting

### Make the Reina Pepiada Filling

1. Mash [avocados]{ ing="ripe-avocados" } until mostly smooth (some texture is good)
1. Fold in [mayonnaise]{ ing="mayonnaise" }, [lime]{ ing="juice-of-1-lime" } juice, [cilantro]{ ing="cilantro" }, [onion]{ ing="red-onion" }, and [jalapeño]{ ing="jalape-o" }
1. Add shredded [chicken]{ ing="shredded-cooked-chicken" } and mix to combine. Season with [salt and pepper]{ ing="salt-and-pepper" }
1. Let rest 15-30 minutes for flavors to meld (the [lime]{ ing="juice-of-1-lime" } juice also keeps [avocado]{ ing="ripe-avocados" } from browning)

### Make the Con Queso Filling

1. Split each warm arepa 3/4 of the way through horizontally (like a pita pocket)
1. Stuff generously with shredded cheese
1. Return to the cast iron or a baking sheet and place in the oven (or under the broiler 2-3 min) until cheese is melted and starting to pull

### Assemble

1. Fill Reina Pepiada arepas with the [chicken]{ ing="shredded-cooked-chicken" }-[avocado]{ ing="ripe-avocados" } mixture and serve immediately
1. Serve Con Queso arepas with optional butter spread inside

## Notes

- **Finding masarepa in the US:** Look for *Harina PAN* (yellow and blue bag) in the Latin/Hispanic aisle of most large grocery stores (Walmart, Target, Kroger) or any Latin market. Goya also makes a version labeled “Masarepa.” Online (Amazon) is reliable if local options fail. In Mexico, Harina PAN is available at supermercados or tiendas de abarrotes focused on Venezuelan/Colombian ingredients — *masa harina (e.g., Maseca) is not a substitute*; it undergoes nixtamalization which produces a distinctly earthy, tortilla-like flavor instead of neutral corn
- **Air fryer alternative:** Brush arepas lightly with oil. Air fry at 370°F for 12-15 minutes, flipping halfway, until golden and hollow-sounding when tapped. No cast iron needed
- **Dough consistency check:** Cracks at the edges when pressing → too dry, add water 1 Tbsp at a time. Won’t hold shape → too wet, add masarepa a tablespoon at a time
- **Hollow tap test:** A dull thud means the center is still raw; a lighter hollow knock means done
- **Con Queso cheese options:** Queso blanco is most authentic and mild; mozzarella melts more smoothly; Oaxacan cheese (quesillo) is stringy and excellent. Avoid pre-shredded bags — the starch coating inhibits melting
- **Make ahead:** Cooked plain arepas keep refrigerated for 3 days or frozen for 1 month. Reheat in a dry cast iron or air fryer at 350°F until warmed through. Make the Reina Pepiada filling up to 1 day ahead (keep avocado mashed with lime juice, covered tightly)
